        "contenuto_esteso": "7 Updates Auto-updates live-orange 10m ago ( 05:50 GMT ) Markets bracing for ‘drawn-out’ war, analyst says Global markets are likely to keep falling after Trump’s speech this week, threatening to bomb Iran “back to the stone ages,” says Eric Golson, a professor of economics at the University of Surrey. “I think the markets are returning to reality now, the reality of a drawn-out, long-term conflict for a while. We’re looking at talking about [bringing the war] to a resolution, which, as far as economists go and markets go, is always a negative,” Golson told Al Jazeera. Government across the globe, he added, “are going to try and make business easier to do now”. “That may give markets a little bit of insurance over the next couple of weeks,” he said. “Probably we will see things still go down, though. Obviously, the problems here are many.” live-orange 22m ago ( 05:37 GMT ) Iran claims it has shot down second F-35 fighter jet The IGRC claim to have shot down a second American F-35 while the warplane was flying over central Iran. A Telegram post from the IGRC’s Fars News Agency said that the jet was “completely destroyed and crashed” and no information was available about the pilot due to the severe damage to the aircraft. Iran’s Mehr News Agency said it was unlikely the pilot managed to eject due to the “violent explosion” from the crash. The US Central Command did not immediately respond to the posts, but disputed an earlier claim from the IGRC that it had shot down a US fighter jet. live-orange 25m ago ( 05:35 GMT ) How war on Iran turned Pakistan’s LNG surplus into a looming shortage Pakistan has been experiencing an energy crisis as a result of the US-Israeli war with Iran. At the start of this year, Pakistan had more imported liquefied natural gas (LNG) than it could use. Demand had been falling for three straight years, from a peak of 8.2 million tonnes in 2021 to 6.1 million tonnes by late 2025, as cheap solar panels flooded the market and factories cut back. For Pakistan, which secures nearly all its imported gas from Qatar and the United Arab Emirates, and holds no emergency reserves, the shift from surplus to shortage happened almost overnight. Read more here . Advertisement live-orange 34m ago ( 05:25 GMT ) What are the Iran-Oman ‘talks’? Iran is in talks with Oman to jointly manage the Strait of Hormuz. The state-run IRNA news agency said the discussions are to draft a “protocol” for the narrow waterway that is a lifeline for the global economy. Iran and Oman sit on opposite sides of the strait where a fifth of the world’s oil and gas transits in peacetime. But since the US-Israel war on Iran began on February 28, Iran has effectively blocked passage for most ships — except those from select countries that have negotiated deals with Tehran. Little is known about the Iran-Oman protocol. It is also unclear whether or not a deal would be acceptable to other countries — in the region and beyond. Other Gulf countries have recently insisted that they are included in any plan for the future of the Strait of Hormuz on which their energy exports depend. live-orange 45m ago ( 05:15 GMT ) Asia markets are responding positively to news of Iran-Oman talks By Patrick Fok It doesn’t seem like we are getting any closer to a resolution, and in fact, in the last 24 hours, Trump has escalated his threat against Iran. Energy prices are also continuing to rise. Brent crude is trading at around $110 a barrel, up 8 percent from earlier, and yet we’re seeing markets reverse some of yesterday’s sharp losses. Now, some markets in the region are closed for the long Easter weekend – including here in Singapore – but some of the main markets that have been affected by the energy crisis are up. South Korea’s KOSPI is up around 3 percent and Japan’s Nikkei 225 is up around 1 percent. It is interesting to see how markets are reacting to the latest developments, particularly the talks between Iran and Oman and reports of a “draft proposal” for transit through the Strait of Hormuz. That’s doing a lot to alleviate concerns about energy supplies in this region, as a lot of Asian countries rely heavily on imports via the Middle East. It also says a lot about what’s driving markets. It appears to be not so much a response to the crisis itself but rather the energy supply situation. These include concerns about energy flows into this part of the world. What we’re seeing now is a reversal of what we had a day before. How long it lasts, how all this is going to work practically remains to be seen, but it’s certainly lifting the mood among investors. live-orange 51m ago ( 05:08 GMT ) Philippine ships granted “safe” passage in Strait of Hormuz By Patrick Fok The Philippines says its ships will be granted safe passage in the Strait of Hormuz. It’s a significant development for the government in Manila and another sign of shipping movement through the strait. The Philippines held talks with Iran after being in contact for some time. Manila wanted to be designated a “non-hostile” state so it could receive shipments of oil. Foreign Minister, Theresa Lazaro, said the talks were productive and positive and their Iranian counterpart pledged that Philippine-flagged vessels, energy and seafarers would be allowed “safe, unhindered and expeditious passage”. live-orange 55m ago ( 05:05 GMT ) Welcome to our coverage Hello, and welcome to our live coverage of the United States and Israel’s war on Iran. Stay with us for the latest news, reactions and analysis from across the Middle East and beyond. You can find our coverage from Thursday, April 2, here ."
